  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Scaling the delivery of the luminos program within government school systems in Liberia and the Gambia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Pandemic-related school closures impacted 250 million students in sub-Saharan Africa, adding to the 100 million children consistently out of school prior to 2020. Hundreds of millions lost not only the opportunity to learn, but the future opportunity to earn, and millions may not set foot back in a classroom. To help get more children back into classrooms, the Luminos Fund developed a program that helps children catch up to grade level and return to school. Through its original model, Luminos trains community-based organizations to identify and recruit out-of-school children and engage them in a ten-month, activity-based curriculum. Luminos then helps children reintegrate into public school systems at age-appropriate grade levels. A 2018 longitudinal study found that around 90 percent of Luminos students transitioned to formal schools at the end of the program and 74 percent were still attending a year later. On average, Luminos students outperformed their peers in literacy and numeracy by ten percentage points. In the 2022-23 school year, with multi-donor funding, Luminos selected IDInsight to conduct a randomized controlled trial (RCT) to rigorously evaluate the programs effectiveness. Support from Development Innovation Ventures will strengthen the ongoing RCT by enabling Luminos to track student enrollment, retention, and learning outcomes for a year beyond the end of the program. DIV will also support Luminos to provide technical assistance to the governments of Liberia and The Gambia to determine how public schools can directly implement the Luminos program. Luminos aims to reach 137,040 children and 2,302 teachers during the award period, and has the potential to reach millions of out-of-school children through government partnerships.</narrative>
  </description>
